From: ViKing <viking.yfy@qq.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:28:52 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: pad short TX packets

QSDK R48 enables NSS_DP_TX_SMALL_PACKET_WAR for EDMA v2/v3 and sets
the minimum transmitted packet size to 49 bytes on IPQ53xx. MAC padding
does not satisfy this EDMA input requirement. Apply the same minimum
before descriptor preparation for ordinary, software-GSO and VP traffic.

Use the non-freeing padding API so every failure keeps the existing skb
ownership contract: ordinary/GSO callers drop it once, while a failed VP
injection returns the skb to its caller. Recompute the descriptor count
after padding, which may linearize a fragmented short skb.

The workaround closes a vendor contract gap; it does not by itself prove
the cause of the observed shared-egress stall.

Signed-off-by: ViKing <viking.yfy@qq.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.c
@@ -724,6 +724,20 @@ static enum edma_tx_status edma_tx_ring_
 	int port_id = port->port_id;
 	u32 hw_next_to_use = 0, start_idx, queue_id;
 
+	/* Match the QSDK EDMA short-packet workaround before DMA mapping.
+	 * Keep ownership with the caller on failure, including VP injection.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(skb->len < EDMA_TX_MIN_PKT_SIZE)) {
+		int ret = __skb_put_padto(skb, EDMA_TX_MIN_PKT_SIZE, false);
+
+		if (ret)
+			return EDMA_TX_FAIL;
+
+		/* Padding can linearize a fragmented short skb. */
+		num_tx_desc_needed = skb_is_nonlinear(skb) ?
+			edma_tx_num_descs_for_sg(skb) : 1;
+	}
+
 	hw_next_to_use = txdesc_ring->prod_idx;
 	start_idx = hw_next_to_use;
 
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.h
@@ -25,6 +25,9 @@
 #define EDMA_TX_RING_SIZE               2048
 #define EDMA_TX_RING_SIZE_MASK		(EDMA_TX_RING_SIZE - 1)
 
+/* EDMA requires at least 49 bytes, even when the MAC pads short frames. */
+#define EDMA_TX_MIN_PKT_SIZE		49
+
 /* Max segment processing capacity of HW for TSO. */
 #define EDMA_TX_TSO_SEG_MAX		32
 
